01:13:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I totally understand your skepticism—that’s exactly the “proof” so many of us were given, and it *feels* like it must mean something. After all, why else would thick edges almost always be at the bottom unless gravity had something to do with it? But once you dig into how medieval window glass was actually made and installed, the pattern stops looking like flow and starts looking like good old-fashioned craftsmanship. Here’s the key: **the thick-at-the-bottom observation is not evidence of flow because it’s a *choice*, not a consequence.** Let’s break down why. ### The old-school manufacturing process made uneven glass *by default* Medieval window glass wasn’t cast into perfect sheets. Two common methods were: - **Crown glass:** A blob of molten glass was blown and spun rapidly into a large, flat disc (like a pizza dough). The disc was thicker at the center (the “bullseye”) and thinner, sometimes rippled, at the edges. When cut into panes, you’d get pieces that were naturally wedge-shaped or variable in thickness. - **Cylinder/broad glass:** A bubble was blown and swung to elongate it into a cylinder. The ends were cut off, the cylinder was slit lengthwise, and it was reheated to flatten it into a sheet. Because it was flattened by gravity and manual manipulation, the resulting sheet had uneven thickness—often thicker along the original seam or in ripples across the surface. So right out of the kiln, these panes were **never uniform**. They had taper, lumps, and wavy textures. ### Glaziers intentionally put the heavy side down When it came time to install, the glazier faced a wobbly, irregular piece. For purely structural and practical reasons, the *heavier*, thicker edge would be placed at the bottom: - **Stability:** A lopsided pane sits more securely in its lead came frame with the weight lower. - **Strength:** The thickest part is best positioned to resist pressure from wind, rain, and the weight of the glass itself. - **Aesthetics:** They could also orient the more transparent part upward for better light, leaving the irregular, rippled thick bit at the base. So *of course* you find many old windows thicker at the bottom—it was the installer’s deliberate choice. But here’s the clincher: **if flow were the cause, you’d never find thick edges at the top or sides.** Yet such windows exist in the same churches. In some panes, the thickest part is at the top or even horizontal, from ancient times when a piece was turned sideways because it fit the frame that way. If glass flowed downward over centuries, these orientations would have slowly shifted—but they haven’t. Measurements on medieval glass show absolutely no detectable geometric change consistent with viscous flow at room temperature. ### The real “coincidence” is a survivorship bias We often point to the thick-bottomed ones because they *look* like evidence. Windows that happen to have thick edges elsewhere are less remarked upon because they don’t fit the story. In reality, a survey of ancient glass panes shows exactly the distribution you’d expect from irregular manufacturing and pragmatic installation—not from a fluid slowly sagging under gravity. ### What about the “supercooled liquid” idea? Glass is technically an amorphous solid that has undergone a glass transition. It’s *kinetically* stuck in a disordered state, but at normal temperatures its atoms aren’t moving enough to allow viscous flow. The myth likely arose from the fact that if you get glass hot enough (near its glass-transition temperature), it *will* flow—but at room temperature you’re looking at a viscosity so high that the time for any appreciable deformation is literally astronomical. We’re talking many, many times the age of the universe just to get a molecule’s width of movement. So no, it’s not a coincidence—it’s a perfectly sensible human practice, baked into the fabrication and framing, that created such a convincing illusion that it fooled generations (including, it seems, a lot of teachers!). The windows haven’t oozed because glass is a solid, and the thick bottom edges are there because medieval glaziers knew exactly what they were doing.
