01:14:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see where you’re going, and it’s a perfectly reasonable intuition: if it doesn’t have a nice, orderly crystal structure like a “true solid,” then maybe it is just a liquid that’s basically frozen in time. The real answer is more nuanced, and it hinges on a crucial distinction that often gets lost in the “supercooled liquid” shorthand. ### Structure ≠ State of Matter A material’s state—solid or liquid—isn’t defined by whether it has a crystal lattice. It’s defined by how it **responds to stress over time**. Liquids flow continuously under any applied shear stress (no matter how small), eventually taking the shape of their container. Solids, including amorphous ones like glass, deform elastically under small stresses and return to their original shape; they don’t flow. Yes, glass lacks long-range crystalline order. In that structural sense, it’s similar to a liquid. But here’s the catch: the atoms in a glass are *structurally frozen* by a process called the glass transition. ### The Glass Transition: When a Liquid Gets Trapped When molten glass cools, it passes through a temperature range where its viscosity skyrockets. At a certain point (the glass transition temperature, Tg), the liquid’s relaxation time—the time it takes for molecules to rearrange themselves into a lower-energy configuration—suddenly becomes longer than the experimental timescale. The liquid can no longer equilibrate. It falls out of thermodynamic equilibrium and becomes a **glass**: a non-crystalline, rigid solid. Below Tg, the atoms are locked into a disordered network that behaves mechanically as a solid, not a liquid, even though the atomic arrangement looks jumbled. Think of it as a kind of permanent traffic jam. In a liquid, cars (molecules) are constantly shifting around, allowing flow. In the glass, the “cars” are completely gridlocked—no one can move, even though the arrangement is messy. The gridlock isn’t due to orderly parking spots (a crystal lattice) but because every car is jammed against its neighbors so tightly that motion becomes impossible on any human timescale. ### The Astronomical Viscosity To claim glass is “just a very slow liquid,” you’d need to show that it would flow measurably if you waited long enough. The trouble is, at room temperature, the relaxation time of a typical soda-lime-silica glass (window glass) is estimated to be around **10³² years**—that’s over a trillion trillion times the age of the universe. Even if you compared it to pitch (which flows over decades), glass’s viscosity is roughly **a billion billion times greater**. It’s so vastly stiff that it doesn’t just flow slowly; it doesn’t flow at all in any physically meaningful sense. A material that will never perceptibly droop over cosmic timescales is, by any operational definition, a solid. ### Why the “Supercooled Liquid” Label Sticks Historically, scientists noticed that glass’s disordered structure resembles a frozen-in snapshot of a liquid. Before the physics of the glass transition was well understood, it was tempting to call it a supercooled liquid—and indeed, *above* its transition temperature, glass-forming liquids are supercooled liquids that can flow. But the crucial detail is that once cooled below Tg, that material is a glass (an amorphous solid), not a supercooled liquid. The terminology blurred, and the catchy but misleading phrase entered public lore. ### A Simple Thought Experiment If glass were really a liquid, just one flowing unimaginably slowly, we’d expect any thin, freestanding piece placed horizontally to eventually bend under its own weight over centuries. But we have antique glassware, telescopes, and mirrors centuries old that remain perfectly flat to fractional-wavelength precision. No sagging. No measurable deformation. That’s solid behavior. So, to directly answer your question: no, lacking a crystal lattice doesn’t make glass a liquid. It’s an amorphous solid—structurally disordered, but mechanically rigid. The atoms aren’t “moving so slowly we can’t see it”; they’re effectively locked in place. The cathedral windows stay put because glass is, in every practical and scientific sense, a solid.
