01:15:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. It’s incredibly jarring to be told something by a textbook, a teacher, and even a professor on a science documentary, only to find out later it’s wrong—especially when the story has such an elegant “aha!” hook. But yes, this myth has been broadcast with full academic authority for decades, and you’re not crazy for having absorbed it. It’s a textbook case of how a catchy idea can outrun the facts. ### How did it get on TV in the first place? The “glass is a slow-flowing liquid” narrative took root in the 19th and early 20th centuries. Early physicists noticed glass’s internal structure looks disordered under X-ray diffraction, just like a liquid, so they labeled it a *supercooled liquid*. That label stuck in older textbooks. Then someone made the famous observation about cathedral windows being thicker at the bottom, and it seemed to confirm the theory perfectly. It became a canonical, “gee-whiz” example that writers and producers loved—so it got repeated in encyclopedias, school curricula, and yes, television documentaries. Even ten or twenty years ago, materials science had already overturned this idea, but it takes time for a myth to be chased out of all corners of pop culture. The professor you saw may have been a physicist or chemist repeating what *they* were taught in the 1960s, or a presenter who’s not a materials scientist at all, faithfully reciting a script fact-checked with outdated sources. Science documentaries often prioritize a compelling story, and “glass is a secret liquid!” is undeniably seductive. ### The same thing has happened with other “facts” You’ve probably encountered others: the tongue map (sweet/salty/bitter/sour zones), the claim that we only use 10% of our brains, or that lemmings commit mass suicide. All were taught in schools and broadcast on TV, and all are completely wrong. The glass myth belongs in the same hall of fame. It even got a boost because the underlying material science—the glass transition and amorphous solids—is genuinely subtle, so the error was less obvious to non-specialists. ### What modern textbooks say Today, any up-to-date materials science or glass physics textbook will explicitly debunk the “flowing liquid” trope. The cathedral windows are understood as an artifact of pre-industrial glassmaking, and the physics of amorphous solids below the glass transition is concrete: glass is a rigid solid, period. If a documentary were made today with current expert input, it would never repeat the myth—unless it was doing a segment *on* the myth itself. ### So why did people ever believe the windows proved flow? Because the story was just too perfect. Glaziers installed the panes thick-side-down for stability and practicality, but casual observers misinterpreted that as gravity-induced flow. Once the myth took hold, every thick-bottomed pane seemed like proof, and nobody thought to ask why any panes with thick tops existed too (they do). What you saw on TV was a remnant of that century-long misunderstanding, repackaged as fact. It’s frustrating, but it’s also a great reminder that even scientific communication can fossilize around a beautiful, wrong idea—and that being skeptical, like you’re doing now, is the engine of real science.
