01:17:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I totally get that. You've seen it with your own eyes: old glass is wavy and uneven, modern glass is perfectly flat. That's a real, observable pattern. And if the only explanation you'd ever heard was "glass slowly flows over time," then that pattern would seem like a smoking gun. Why *wouldn't* you trust your own eyes? But here's the crucial thing: **the pattern is real, but the cause is completely different.** You've stumbled onto a classic case of correlation not implying causation. Let me explain what's actually going on—it's even more interesting, I promise. ### The "waviness" is a manufacturing fingerprint, not a sign of age The uneven, rippled glass you've handled—whether in antique bottles, old window panes, or vintage mirrors—isn't wavy because it *became* that way over centuries. It's wavy because it was *born* that way. The old manufacturing methods I mentioned earlier (crown glass, cylinder glass) produced sheets that were inherently imperfect: variations in thickness, subtle curves, trapped bubbles, surface ripples. These imperfections were frozen in the moment the glass cooled and hardened. A freshly made pane in 1650 looked just as wavy as it does today. No flow required. Modern glass, by contrast, is overwhelmingly made using the **float glass process**, invented in the 1950s. Molten glass is floated on a bath of molten tin, creating perfectly flat, uniform sheets with parallel surfaces. That's why your new windows and modern bottles are optically near-perfect—not because they're "young" and haven't had time to flow, but because the manufacturing technology is fundamentally different. So yes, you've noticed a genuine pattern: older glass looks wavy, newer glass looks flat. But it's a historical timeline of manufacturing techniques, not of physical flow. An ancient Egyptian glass amulet looks the same today as it did when it was made, irregularities and all. ### What you'd actually see if glass flowed If glass flowed at roo