en you find something that breaks your old categories, you make better categories, not worse ones. By your standard, you could argue that scientists invented "plate tectonics" just to avoid admitting the continents don't move, or "germ theory" to avoid admitting disease was a mystery. New categories arise when old ones fail. ### Think about other "amorphous solids" in daily life Obsidian (volcanic glass) is natural glass. If you chip a piece of obsidian, it's been sitting around for millions of years, completely amorphous. According to the "it's a liquid" idea, it should have slumped or crystallized over that time. It hasn't. It's still a razor-sharp, rigid rock. What word works better for that object as you hold it: "liquid" or "solid"? Even a child would call it a solid, because it acts like one. All "amorphous" adds is: it doesn't have the neat internal order of a crystal. That's not fence-sitting; it's just accurate description. ### So why does "amorphous solid" feel like a cheat? Because the myth was so beautifully simple: "It's a liquid, just slow." That's a one-liner. It's memorable. "Amorphous solid below its glass transition temperature" is a mouthful. It feels like a downgrade from that elegant, quirky fact. But the universe doesn't owe us elegant one-liners. Sometimes the truth is that nature is more complex than a binary "solid vs. liquid" can capture, and we have to build a slightly larger vocabulary to describe it accurately. "Amorphous solid" isn't fence-sitting. It's the admission that the fence was never just a line between two fields—there was a whole third field we hadn't noticed before. Glass lives there. It's not "kind of a liquid." It's a solid that happens to have a jumbled-up structure—and that structural quirk has exactly zero effect on whether it will hold its shape under your coffee mug. It will. Because it's a solid. I know this can feel like a verbal trick. But I promise it's the opposite: it's an attempt to make words match what the mat